// Developmental vascular morphology is separated from functioning adult perfusion.
study('gerhardt2003-retinal-blockade', 'gerhardt2003-vegf-guidance', 'Developing postnatal mouse retinal vascular plexus',
  'P5 eyes receive approximately 0.5 microlitre of 1 microgram/microlitre soluble Flt-1/Fc or receptor-neutralizing reagent; retinas are examined six hours later by whole-mount vascular staining. Reflux makes delivered dose variable. VEGFR1 blockade and control reagents are distinct comparisons.',
  'Tip-cell filopodial extension and plexus spreading, not tissue oxygen tension or functional organ output.',
  'VEGF sequestration or VEGFR2 blockade retracts filopodia and limits spreading, whereas tested VEGFR1 blockade does not produce the same morphology.',
  ['Six hours is the earliest examined time, not a measured universal response delay. Retinal endpoint morphology does not isolate every adhesion or intracellular pathway.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('retinal-vegf-blockade-input', 'Retinal VEGF/VEGFR perturbation', 'gerhardt2003-retinal-blockade', 'The intervention changes available VEGF-A or receptor recognition within an already developing retinal plexus; it does not construct endothelium from unorganized material.');
observation('retinal-filopodia-response', 'Source-bound sprouting response', 'gerhardt2003-retinal-blockade', 'At six hours, the tested VEGF trap and VEGFR2 blockade reduce tip-cell filopodia and plexus spreading; VEGFR1 blockade is a pathway-specific negative comparison.');
operating('retinal-vegf-blockade-input', 'retinal-filopodia-response', 'gerhardt2003-retinal-blockade', 'Changing VEGF availability or VEGFR2 signaling changes the measured retinal sprouting morphology within this preparation.', 'P5 retinal endothelial tips and their existing scaffold', 'Specified intraocular VEGF trap or receptor antibody', 'Six-hour filopodial and plexus-spreading morphology', 'Neither a universal angiogenesis requirement nor a direct oxygen-delivery gain is inferred.');
study('gerhardt2003-ligand-distribution', 'gerhardt2003-vegf-guidance', 'Mouse retinal angiogenesis with altered VEGF distribution',
  'Compare wild-type and VEGF120-only or lens-overexpression preparations, alongside acute VEGF-related ligand additions; confocal morphology and proliferation markers distinguish tip and stalk responses. These interventions differ in genotype, spatial expression and ligand context.',
  'Filopodial orientation, branching, vessel diameter and stalk proliferation.',
  'Flattened/ectopic VEGF distributions can reduce directed spreading while enlarging vessels or producing chaotic fusion; more proliferative signaling does not guarantee a well-patterned network.',
  ['Distinct genetic and acute manipulations are not a matched scalar dose series. A common exact migration/proliferation coupling law is not identified.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,1,2]);
observation('vascular-guidance-versus-growth', 'Vascular guidance differs from growth', 'gerhardt2003-ligand-distribution', 'The reported patterning contrasts separate directed tip migration from stalk proliferation: enlarged or proliferating vascular structures need not have normal directed branching.');
observation('vascular-lumen-tip-boundary', 'Perfused lumen stops before sprout tip', 'gerhardt2003-ligand-distribution', 'Retinal dextran perfusion labels lumen up to, but not within, the leading tip cell; proliferation labeling is concentrated behind sampled tips. Morphological sprouting, patent lumen and completed tissue perfusion are distinct.');

study('lucitti2007-cardiac-flow', 'lucitti2007-yolk-sac-flow', 'Early mouse whole-embryo/yolk-sac circulation',
  'Tg(epsilon-globin-GFP) Mlc2a-null and littermate embryos collected at 8.5 dpc are staged by somites. Erythroblast tracking and fluorescein-dextran FRAP assess circulation; 8-9-somite plasma measurements are made blind before genotyping.',
  'Erythroblast trajectories and FRAP-derived plasma perfusion coefficients.',
  'Mutants show strongly impaired forward erythroblast movement and lower but nonzero plasma transport; the presence of blood cells in a vessel is not evidence of normal through-flow.',
  ['FRAP coefficient is a preparation-specific transport estimate, not an absolute tissue oxygen-delivery measurement. Somite staging and time-lapse acquisition differ from static blood-cell localization.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,1]);
observation('cardiac-contractility-context', 'Cardiac genotype and flow preparation', 'lucitti2007-cardiac-flow', 'Mlc2a-null embryos provide a genetic cardiac-contractility perturbation with flow tracked in the connected yolk sac; the vascular consequence is secondary to the whole preparation, not a purified endothelial knockout.');
observation('circulation-not-cell-presence', 'Blood presence differs from through-flow', 'lucitti2007-cardiac-flow', 'Blood cells can occupy the mutant plexus while moving slowly back and forth, and plasma transport remains measurable. An anatomical connected channel does not guarantee normal circulation.');
study('lucitti2007-viscosity-rescue', 'lucitti2007-yolk-sac-flow', 'Cultured 8.5-dpc mouse embryos with low circulating hematocrit',
  'Polymerized acrylamide sequesters erythroblasts in blood islands; separate acrylamide-only, TEMED-only and untreated controls are retained. Approximately 1-2 hours after polymerization, a low-hematocrit group receives intracardiac 6% hetastarch solution. Embryos develop for 24 hours in roller culture; remodeling/turning are scored blind.',
  'Yolk-sac area, ordered remodeling/turning scores, retained plasma flow and endothelial immunostaining.',
  'Low-hematocrit embryos retain plasma transport but remodel poorly; increasing viscosity rescues substantial vessel remodeling and eNOS staining without releasing erythroblasts. The mechanosensory force and complete pathway remain unresolved.',
  ['Table 2 rescue-group mean turning remains below untreated control. Absence of a significant comparison is not an equivalence test or proof of full normalization. Oxygen diffusion, soluble signaling and embryo-culture resources still exist.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('low-hematocrit-input', 'Erythroblast sequestration', 'lucitti2007-viscosity-rescue', 'The low-hematocrit manipulation removes most circulating erythroblasts while preserving a comparable measured range of plasma transport, separating circulating-cell contribution from the mere presence of flow.');
observation('viscosity-restoration-input', 'Viscosity restoration with cells sequestered', 'lucitti2007-viscosity-rescue', 'The hetastarch intervention increases fluid viscosity in the already circulating low-hematocrit preparation while erythroblasts remain confined to blood islands.');
observation('vascular-remodeling-rescue', 'Bounded mechanical rescue of remodeling', 'lucitti2007-viscosity-rescue', 'Hetastarch restores substantial hierarchical yolk-sac remodeling, area and eNOS signal relative to low hematocrit. This supports a mechanical contribution, without proving universal oxygen dispensability, exact rescue equivalence or a unique shear-sensing mechanism.');
operating('viscosity-restoration-input', 'vascular-remodeling-rescue', 'lucitti2007-viscosity-rescue', 'Restoring effective viscosity in the low-hematocrit embryo preparation rescues the reported vascular-remodeling endpoints.', 'Existing embryonic yolk-sac vessels exposed to maintained plasma circulation', 'Intracardiac hetastarch in erythroblast-sequestered embryos', '24-hour blinded remodeling/area and eNOS observations', 'Rescue of these endpoints is not a complete construction rule, direct force measurement or proof that all embryonic functions normalize.');

study('hall2014-slice-dilation', 'hall2014-brain-mural-flow', 'Juvenile rat cerebellar slices with retained mural cells',
  'P10-P21 cerebellar slices are superfused at 31-35 degrees C with 20% or 95% oxygen; noradrenaline supplies preconstriction, followed by glutamate or parallel-fiber stimulation. EP4 blockade and TTX test selected response components. Nonconstricting capillaries and selected stimulation-induced constrictions are excluded as described in Methods.',
  'Diameter near identified mural-cell locations; separate patch-current acquisition is not a flow or in-vivo readout.',
  'Neural/glutamate stimulation dilates selected preconstricted capillaries; oxygenation and EP4/NO-related perturbations change the response.',
  ['Selection for noradrenaline responsiveness and pharmacological preconstriction limit generalization. Drug effects do not identify uniquely whether neurons, astrocytes or mural cells are the initial ligand targets.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('slice-neurovascular-input', 'Slice neurovascular stimulation', 'hall2014-slice-dilation', 'The input is a specified glutamate or fiber-stimulation protocol acting on an intact slice with experimentally established vascular tone, rather than isolated direct glutamate action on a single pericyte.');
observation('slice-capillary-dilation', 'Oxygen- and pathway-dependent slice dilation', 'hall2014-slice-dilation', 'Selected capillaries dilate to stimulation under the reported tone/oxygen conditions; TTX and EP4 perturbations constrain the pathway, while the source does not assign a unique initial responding cell.');
operating('slice-neurovascular-input', 'slice-capillary-dilation', 'hall2014-slice-dilation', 'Specified stimulation produces a bounded diameter response in the selected rat-slice capillaries.', 'Preconstricted mural-cell-associated capillary segments in rat cerebellar slices', 'Glutamate or parallel-fiber stimulation within its specified protocol', 'Optically measured local vessel diameter', 'No transfer of this ex-vivo response to every adult vessel or direct pericyte receptor mechanism is admitted.');
study('hall2014-whisker-vessels', 'hall2014-brain-mural-flow', 'Adult anesthetized mouse somatosensory cortex',
  'Adult NG2-DsRed mice are ventilated and maintained under alpha-chloralose after surgery; physiological blood gases/pressure and temperature are monitored. Electrical infraorbital whisker-pathway stimulation uses 3-Hz, 2- or 15-second trains. Two-photon imaging identifies vessel branch order and local mural-cell position; responding vessels exceed the source 5% diameter criterion.',
  'Vessel dilation magnitude/timing and separate red-cell-velocity measurements.',
  'The source reports earlier first-order capillary than penetrating-arteriole dilation and larger responses at labeled mural-cell locations, interpreted as active pericyte dilation.',
  ['Anesthesia, marker/morphology classification, local vessel geometry and image smoothing enter the attribution. Observed sensory stimulation-to-diameter response does not uniquely prove cell-selective pericyte causality; Hill uses different classification and preparation.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('hall-sensory-perfusion-response', 'Sensory-evoked vascular timing in Hall', 'hall2014-whisker-vessels', 'The reported sensory preparation shows dilation in multiple vessel orders, including early capillary responses near NG2-labeled mural cells; the pericyte attribution is a source interpretation with explicit classification boundaries.');
observation('hall-pericyte-attribution-boundary', 'Hall mural attribution is conditional', 'hall2014-whisker-vessels', 'Branch order and labeled soma/process proximity identify tested locations, while anesthesia and the capillary definition differ from the later smooth-muscle-marker comparison. The two studies are not interchangeable quantitative repeats.');
study('hill2015-optical-mural-response', 'hill2015-brain-mural-flow', 'Mouse cerebral mural-cell stimulation with SMA-resolved vessel classification',
  'NG2-Cre/ChR2-YFP and SMA-mCherry reporters distinguish circumferential smooth-muscle-covered segments from longitudinal pericyte-covered segments. Confocal local line scanning measures diameter; localized two-photon activation limits upstream optical activation in separate velocity measurements. Main Methods specify 4-16-week males and females and awake or ketamine/xylazine states as indicated; not all assay-specific state assignments are reconstructed here.',
  'Diameter and red-cell velocity in separate targeted optical acquisitions.',
  'Direct optical activation constricts smooth-muscle-covered arteriolar/precapillary segments, while measured SMA-negative pericyte segments lack comparable rapid constriction or velocity reduction.',
  ['Negative responses are finite-resolution/time-window findings, not proof of a universal zero. The reporter also labels non-mural populations, addressed partly by sparse-induction controls whose full supplement was not independently read.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('mural-optical-activation-input', 'SMA-resolved local optical input', 'hill2015-optical-mural-response', 'ChR2 activation is applied at morphologically and SMA-classified vascular sites; small vessel diameter or early branch order alone does not distinguish smooth muscle from capillary pericytes.');
observation('mural-contractility-positive-and-null', 'Mural-cell positive and null contrasts', 'hill2015-optical-mural-response', 'The study detects rapid constriction and reduced red-cell velocity in smooth-muscle-covered segments, with no comparable measured response in the specified SMA-negative pericyte segments. The null is bounded to this protocol.');
operating('mural-optical-activation-input', 'mural-contractility-positive-and-null', 'hill2015-optical-mural-response', 'Local optical input has different measured vascular responses across the source-defined mural-cell classes.', 'Reporter- and morphology-classified mouse cerebral microvessels', 'Local ChR2 stimulation in the source imaging protocols', 'Vessel diameter and separately acquired red-cell velocity', 'A class-specific experimental response does not resolve all cross-paper terminology, slow responses or every pericyte function.');
study('hill2015-awake-whisker-response', 'hill2015-brain-mural-flow', 'Awake head-fixed mouse somatosensory cortex',
  'Following acute cranial-window surgery and recovery from anesthesia, NG2-Cre/GCaMP3 mice receive 30 seconds of 5-Hz, 100-ms whisker air puffs. Vascular diameter and neural/mural calcium are measured in smooth-muscle-positive and negative segments.',
  'Calcium and vessel dilation time courses, with distinct segment and cell denominators.',
  'Robust prompt dilation occurs in SMC-covered segments; SMA-negative capillary segments show only a small delayed change interpreted as passive, without a matched pericyte-calcium response.',
  ['Figure 6 legend and main prose report differing calcium-cell counts; no harmonized animal/vessel manifest is available. Source nulls do not establish exact equality or absence of all slower microvascular control.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('hill-awake-neurovascular-response', 'Awake sensory response favors SMC segments', 'hill2015-awake-whisker-response', 'The awake sensory protocol reports prompt smooth-muscle-associated dilation and calcium decrease; the small delayed capillary change is not treated as direct active pericyte contraction.');
observation('perfusion-mechanism-unresolved', 'Pericyte versus smooth-muscle attribution remains scoped', 'hill2015-awake-whisker-response', 'Hall and Hill differ in anesthetic state, stimulation, classification and selection. These contrasts leave a mechanistic boundary; shared diameter or branch order does not establish an identical effector population.');
// Neural signals, endocrine output and restoration of a regulated variable are separate endpoints.
study('tan2016-warmth-activity', 'tan2016-warmth-coordination', 'Awake mouse preoptic thermoregulatory neurons',
  'Cre-dependent GCaMP6s in VMPO PACAP neurons is monitored by fiber photometry during controlled ambient ramps/steps. Reporter-negative and nearby-population controls distinguish selected optical artifacts and cell populations; slice sensor validation is a separate acquisition.',
  'Population calcium fluorescence during environmental warmth/cold challenges.',
  'VMPO PACAP neurons respond rapidly and preferentially to ambient warmth in the tested range, consistent with upstream peripheral thermal information rather than demonstrated intrinsic heat sensing.',
  ['Calcium population fluorescence is neither single-cell spike count nor direct thermoregulatory output. Similar final response during ramps/steps does not identify one universal tuning or transfer function.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('warmth-environmental-input', 'Controlled ambient warmth', 'tan2016-warmth-activity', 'Controlled ambient temperature changes act on the entire mouse, including peripheral sensors; they are not direct heating of genetically identified isolated neurons.');
observation('warmth-neural-response', 'Warm-sensitive population response', 'tan2016-warmth-activity', 'The selected VMPO population increases calcium activity within seconds during warmth, while tested cold/unrelated stimuli do not show the same activation. Intrinsic sensing and complete afferent circuitry remain unresolved.');
study('tan2016-autonomic-stimulation', 'tan2016-warmth-coordination', 'Mouse VMPO PACAP/BDNF stimulation and thermoregulatory effectors',
  'Cre-dependent stabilized step-function opsin in VMPO PACAP or BDNF populations is activated by brief blue-light stimulation (2 Hz for 30 seconds, reported 10 mW), with no-opsin controls. Core rectal temperature, tail infrared readings and subcutaneous interscapular temperature are recorded using distinct instruments.',
  'Core, tail-base and brown-fat-associated temperature time courses.',
  'Stimulation lowers core and interscapular temperature while raising tail temperature, consistent with reciprocal heat-production/heat-loss regulation. The response persists on the opsin timescale and returns toward baseline.',
  ['Tail temperature is an indirect vasodilation readout, and interscapular subcutaneous temperature is a proxy for BAT thermogenesis rather than direct ATP turnover. Artificial sufficiency does not prove population necessity or a closed quantitative homeostat.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('thermal-neural-stimulation-input', 'Brief VMPO stimulation', 'tan2016-autonomic-stimulation', 'The optogenetic intervention increases excitability of an identified existing preoptic population, with no-opsin illuminated mice as controls.');
observation('coordinated-thermal-effectors', 'Coordinated thermal readouts', 'tan2016-autonomic-stimulation', 'Core/interscapular cooling and tail warming co-occur under the tested stimulation protocol. This is bounded organ-effector coordination; exact vascular conductance, heat flux and all temperature-feedback paths are unmeasured.');
operating('thermal-neural-stimulation-input', 'coordinated-thermal-effectors', 'tan2016-autonomic-stimulation', 'Activating the specified VMPO population changes multiple measured thermoregulatory endpoints in the same in-vivo preparation.', 'Existing VMPO PACAP/BDNF-expressing circuit and mouse thermal effectors', 'Specified brief SSFO light protocol', 'Rectal core, tail infrared and interscapular temperature measurements', 'Proxy readouts and sufficiency are retained without complete feedback closure, universal set point or neural necessity.');

study('zimmerman2016-systemic-challenge', 'zimmerman2016-thirst-anticipation', 'Adult mouse SFO Nos1 population under fluid-balance perturbation',
  'Adult Nos1-Cre mice of both sexes express GCaMP6s in SFO. Hypertonic saline/mannitol, PEG-related hypovolemia or isoproterenol-related hypotension are applied in distinct trials. Losartan and captopril distinguish selected angiotensin-dependent responses. Photometry uses pre-established salt-response and placement exclusions.',
  'Population calcium response to source-specific volume, pressure and osmotic challenges.',
  'SFO activity responds to multiple homeostatic perturbations; angiotensin blockers remove selected volume/pressure-associated responses while leaving the osmotic response relatively preserved.',
  ['Challenge classes differ in route, dose and kinetics. Neural fluorescence alone is not successful restoration of blood pressure, volume or osmolarity.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('fluid-balance-perturbation-input', 'Distinct systemic fluid challenges', 'zimmerman2016-systemic-challenge', 'The source applies chemically and physiologically distinct perturbations; none is a generic scalar stress input with a common inferred weight.');
observation('fluid-signal-pathway-separation', 'Multiple fluid signals converge conditionally', 'zimmerman2016-systemic-challenge', 'Specified osmotic and volume/pressure challenges engage SFO Nos1 activity through partly separable angiotensin-dependent and independent routes; no single required upstream hormone covers all tested inputs.');
study('zimmerman2016-drinking-dynamics', 'zimmerman2016-thirst-anticipation', 'Water-restricted or salt-challenged mouse drinking',
  'SFO photometry follows drinking after deprivation or salt challenge, with water/saline, empty bottle and oral-cooling comparisons. Plasma osmolality/protein are separately sampled at specified times in matched protocols; they are not simultaneous continuous measures in every imaged animal.',
  'Licks and SFO fluorescence, compared with separate plasma measurements.',
  'Drinking rapidly inhibits SFO activity before systemic normalization. Hypertonic saline initially inhibits but activity rebounds after about a minute; expectation/air licking alone does not reproduce the full water response.',
  ['Early sensory prediction is distinguished from actual homeostatic correction. Separate plasma samples do not establish individual-trial quantitative coupling, and the precise delayed tonicity sensor is not isolated.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('anticipation-precedes-blood-correction', 'Anticipation precedes blood correction', 'zimmerman2016-drinking-dynamics', 'The measured SFO/lick dynamics change well before the separately sampled blood readouts normalize, so behavioral satiation cannot be equated with already restored internal composition.');
observation('saline-prediction-reversal', 'Early inhibition can be revised', 'zimmerman2016-drinking-dynamics', 'Hypertonic saline causes a transient early inhibition followed by rebound, separating immediate ingestion-linked signals from delayed information about fluid composition. This is not a permanently fixed homeostatic set point.');
study('zimmerman2016-sfo-silencing', 'zimmerman2016-thirst-anticipation', 'Dehydrated mouse SFO inhibition during drinking access',
  'After 24-hour water restriction, eArch3.0-expressing SFO Nos1 neurons are inhibited using 532-nm light (10-15 mW) during 15-minute water access; trials with and without illumination are interleaved, followed by light-off observation. Reporter controls and separate feeding comparisons constrain behavioral specificity.',
  'Water consumption during inhibition and resumed drinking after light removal.',
  'SFO silencing strongly suppresses drinking during the tested access period, followed by renewed drinking when inhibition ends.',
  ['The temporary intervention does not define every route to drinking, structural nervous-system formation or a universal need for cognition. Randomization/blinding were not used in the reported study.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('sfo-inhibition-input', 'Reversible SFO inhibition', 'zimmerman2016-sfo-silencing', 'The neural perturbation is imposed during an established dehydrated state and access to water, with interleaved illumination conditions.');
observation('drinking-suppression-recovery', 'Suppressed drinking with light-off recovery', 'zimmerman2016-sfo-silencing', 'Water consumption is suppressed during the defined inhibition period and resumes after light removal. The bounded reversible behavioral output is distinct from proof of completed physiological rehydration.');
operating('sfo-inhibition-input', 'drinking-suppression-recovery', 'zimmerman2016-sfo-silencing', 'Silencing the defined SFO population changes drinking during the source access protocol.', 'Adult water-restricted mouse SFO Nos1 neurons and drinking behavior', 'eArch3.0 optical inhibition during water availability', 'Timed water consumption and post-inhibition resumption', 'No claim is made that every fluid-balance process, endocrine pathway or neural architecture is abolished.');

study('mandel2017-vasopressin-water', 'mandelblatcerf2017-vasopressin-anticipation', 'Genetically identified mouse posterior-pituitary-projecting vasopressin neurons',
  'Optically identified individual SON VPpp neurons are recorded electrophysiologically during water-predicting cues and drinking after water restriction. Separate freely moving photometry trials compare water availability across dehydration and food-induced hyperosmolar contexts. Detailed surgeries, full sex/age allocation and calcium processing reside in unread Supplemental Experimental Procedures.',
  'Identified neuronal spikes and separately acquired population calcium aligned to cue/ingestion.',
  'Water-predicting cues reduce VPpp activity before water ingestion, with further decreases during drinking; empty-bowl responses are transient and state dependence matters.',
  ['No simultaneous plasma vasopressin concentration, collecting-duct flux or urine excretion is measured in the admitted observation. Optical tagging establishes cell identity, not a perturbation proving the cue-to-kidney pathway.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('vasopressin-neuron-anticipation', 'VP-neuron anticipatory activity', 'mandel2017-vasopressin-water', 'Identified VPpp firing and separate population recordings decrease following water cues before ingestion; early activity is a predictive neural endpoint rather than observed kidney correction.');
observation('renal-output-inference-boundary', 'Neural activity is not renal output', 'mandel2017-vasopressin-water', 'The interpretation that rapid VPpp activity changes alter circulating vasopressin and renal water retention relies on additional physiology cited by the authors. This case itself does not measure the complete neural-to-hormone-to-kidney path.');
study('mandel2017-vasopressin-food', 'mandelblatcerf2017-vasopressin-anticipation', 'Mouse VPpp population during dry-food ingestion',
  'Food-restricted mice receive a familiar food-associated bowl, large or small chow pellets, or matched non-food objects while VPpp population calcium is recorded. The paper contrasts cue period with feeding onset; detailed Supplemental Experimental Procedures remain unread.',
  'Population calcium in the seconds following cue or food ingestion.',
  'Dry-food ingestion rapidly increases VPpp activity, whereas predictive food cues do not show the robust pre-ingestive response seen for water cues.',
  ['A cue null is not proof that food predictions are impossible under all training or food compositions. Dry chow and water create distinct demands; no common sign or time constant is imposed.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('vasopressin-bidirectional-ingestion', 'Bidirectional input-dependent VP response', 'mandel2017-vasopressin-food', 'Food ingestion and water ingestion have opposite reported activity responses, but only the water condition has a robust pre-ingestive predictive-cue response in the tested training context.');
observation('neuroendocrine-fast-slow-boundary', 'Endocrine architecture includes rapid neural dynamics', 'mandel2017-vasopressin-food', 'Neuroendocrine neuron activity changes on a seconds timescale. Calling every hormonal process intrinsically slow would conflate signal source, circulation, receptor action and measured endpoint.');

study('walker2012-crh-dose-acquisition', 'walker2012-hpa-oscillations', 'Freely moving adult male rat pituitary-adrenal response',
  'Approximately 250-g male Sprague-Dawley rats recover five days after double jugular cannulation. Constant CRH at 0.5, 1.0 or 2.5 micrograms/hour, or saline, is infused from 07:00 to 13:00; corticosterone is sampled every five minutes from 06:00. Doses and time of day are preparation conditions, not universal endocrine thresholds.',
  'Plasma corticosterone radioimmunoassay time series.',
  'Low constant CRH produces persistent oscillatory corticosterone, while higher tested doses produce elevated relatively damped responses.',
  ['Endogenous hypothalamic input is reduced by circadian timing, not anatomically eliminated. Constant exogenous drive does not prove complete central independence or uniquely identify every feedback mechanism.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('constant-crh-input', 'Constant CRH at specified doses', 'walker2012-crh-dose-acquisition', 'The imposed input remains constant over six hours; multiple dose groups test whether peripheral hormone output must simply copy an externally pulsed command.');
observation('dose-dependent-endocrine-regime', 'Oscillatory versus damped hormone response', 'walker2012-crh-dose-acquisition', 'Corticosterone oscillations can occur under the tested constant lower CRH drive, while larger constant doses damp oscillatory output. A fixed fast-neural/slow-hormonal ordering or requirement for pulsatile hypothalamic input is therefore not assumed.');
operating('constant-crh-input', 'dose-dependent-endocrine-regime', 'walker2012-crh-dose-acquisition', 'Constant CRH at different tested doses changes the measured temporal regime of corticosterone secretion.', 'Existing rat pituitary-adrenal system with intact physiological context', 'Six-hour constant intravenous CRH or saline', 'Five-minute sampled plasma corticosterone', 'The experiment does not uniquely prove a deterministic oscillator mechanism, a universal bifurcation threshold or loss of all neural influence.');
study('walker2012-acth-cort-timing', 'walker2012-hpa-oscillations', 'Rat ACTH and corticosterone acquisition during 0.5-microgram/hour CRH',
  'One protocol samples both hormones every 20 minutes during infusion; a distinct initial-pulse protocol samples every 5 minutes over 25 minutes; separate sacrificed cohorts provide trunk blood at 10-minute intervals over 70 minutes. These are different units and cannot be concatenated into one within-animal high-frequency record.',
  'ACTH immunoradiometric and corticosterone radioimmunoassay temporal response.',
  'Both hormones oscillate at coarser sampling, while finer initial/cohort measurements show ACTH rises before corticosterone. The 20-minute series cannot itself resolve the small predicted phase shift.',
  ['Cross-animal decapitation cohorts are not longitudinal single-animal pulses. Hormone lag is consistent with synthesis delay but does not directly isolate its molecular cause.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('endocrine-sampling-phase-boundary', 'Sampling limits endocrine phase inference', 'walker2012-acth-cort-timing', 'Apparent coordination depends on assay interval and unit: the short initial series and sacrificed cohorts support ordered hormone onset, whereas the longer 20-minute series lacks resolution for the predicted short phase delay.');
study('walker2012-oscillator-interpretation', 'walker2012-hpa-oscillations', 'Computational interpretation of pituitary-adrenal dynamics',
  'The paper displays simulations of a prior feedback model and applies detrending, discrete Fourier transforms and short-time spectra to acquired corticosterone data. The prior model derivation, numerical code and raw hormone traces were not rerun.',
  'Model trajectories and derived dominant spectral frequency, not a new independent animal intervention.',
  'Simulations predict oscillatory versus damped output under different constant CRH levels; observed spectral consistency supports compatibility with that model but does not uniquely establish deterministic feedback as the only mechanism.',
  ['Spectral matching to a previously formulated model is not independent replication. A nonsignificant frequency difference is not an equivalence test, and no numerical replay or construction rule is admitted.'], 'computational-analysis', [0,2]);
observation('hpa-model-versus-mechanism', 'Oscillator model remains an interpretation', 'walker2012-oscillator-interpretation', 'The proposed pituitary-adrenal feedback oscillator is compatible with the displayed experiments; neither a fitted diagram nor similar dominant frequencies uniquely identify the complete biological generator.');
study('stavreva2009-array-dynamics', 'stavreva2009-glucocorticoid-pulses', 'Engineered mouse mammary-adenocarcinoma-derived GR reporter cells',
  '3617/3617-ChRFP-NF1 cells carry a large MMTV promoter array and fluorescent GR/locus markers. Corticosterone or dexamethasone is applied at 100 nM for 20 minutes followed by 40-minute withdrawal; imaging and FRAP measure locus association. Endogenous-locus ChIP in 3134 cells is a related but separate assay, not an independent whole-organ result.',
  'Receptor localization and promoter-array exchange after ligand addition/withdrawal.',
  'Natural-ligand withdrawal rapidly reduces GR-array association, while the tested synthetic dexamethasone does not disengage on the same timescale.',
  ['An amplified reporter array differs from single-copy endogenous chromatin. Ligand binding, nuclear residence, chromatin association and epigenetic-state persistence are different quantities.'], 'primary-experiment', [0]);
observation('gr-ligand-waveform-input', 'Specified GR ligand and withdrawal', 'stavreva2009-array-dynamics', 'The experimental input specifies ligand identity, concentration and washout schedule, rather than a generic endocrine signal with one mandatory timescale.');
observation('gr-ligand-specific-dynamics', 'Ligand-dependent GR promoter dynamics', 'stavreva2009-array-dynamics', 'The measured GR-array response follows natural corticosterone withdrawal more rapidly than dexamethasone withdrawal. Nuclear receptor presence does not by itself imply continued productive promoter engagement.');
operating('gr-ligand-waveform-input', 'gr-ligand-specific-dynamics', 'stavreva2009-array-dynamics', 'Ligand identity and withdrawal schedule alter the reported GR-promoter-array association dynamics.', 'Specified engineered mouse mammary-tumor-derived reporter cells', 'Corticosterone/dexamethasone addition and withdrawal', 'Fluorescent GR-array localization and exchange', 'No universal transcription output, endogenous whole-genome response or stable epigenetic memory is inferred.');
study('stavreva2009-transcription-waveform', 'stavreva2009-glucocorticoid-pulses', '3134 mouse mammary-adenocarcinoma-derived cells',
  'Cells in charcoal-stripped serum receive eight 100-nM corticosterone pulses of 20 minutes separated by 40-minute hormone-free intervals, or constant 100-nM treatment. Nascent intron/exon-spanning qPCR and mature RNA assays are normalized within their reported protocols.',
  'Nascent and mature RNA from selected GR-regulated genes.',
  'Pulsed treatment produces cyclic nascent transcription, whereas constant exposure sustains transcription and greater selected mature-RNA accumulation.',
  ['Equal peak concentration is not equal exposure: constant treatment gives three times the scheduled hormone-present duration. The comparison does not isolate waveform independently of cumulative exposure or prove every target requires pulses. No chromatin-modification inheritance is measured.'], 'primary-experiment', [1]);
observation('glucocorticoid-transcription-input', 'Pulse versus constant hormone protocol', 'stavreva2009-transcription-waveform', 'The compared schedules differ in pulse structure and integrated exposure; their biological comparison must preserve both differences.');
observation('hormone-transcription-pulsing', 'Pulsed selected-gene output', 'stavreva2009-transcription-waveform', 'Selected nascent RNAs track the pulse/washout schedule, while mature transcript accumulation differs from constant treatment. Hormone concentration alone is insufficient to identify this temporal response.');
operating('glucocorticoid-transcription-input', 'hormone-transcription-pulsing', 'stavreva2009-transcription-waveform', 'The defined hormone schedules yield different measured transcriptional outputs in 3134 cells.', 'Cultured 3134 cells and specified GR target genes', 'Eight pulse/washout cycles versus constant corticosterone', 'Nascent/mature qPCR readouts at their reported times', 'Waveform and exposure are jointly changed; a frequency-only law or universal necessary gene-programming mechanism is not admitted.');
study('stavreva2009-protein-exposure', 'stavreva2009-glucocorticoid-pulses', '3134-cell protein endpoint under distinct corticosterone schedules',
  'For the Western-blot comparison, 600-nM corticosterone is delivered constantly for 8 hours or in eight 20-minute pulses separated by 40-minute withdrawal. This concentration is distinct from the 100-nM transcription series.',
  'Fkbp5 protein immunoblot relative to the reported loading control.',
  'Constant treatment produces greater Fkbp5 protein accumulation than the reported pulsatile treatment.',
  ['The protein comparison is not the same dose as the principal nascent-RNA series, and cumulative exposure differs. A selected protein band is not whole-organ physiological maladaptation.'], 'primary-experiment', [1]);
observation('hormone-rna-protein-boundary', 'RNA and protein contexts differ', 'stavreva2009-protein-exposure', 'The Fkbp5 protein contrast uses 600 nM and an 8-hour endpoint, so it cannot be silently merged with the 100-nM RNA kinetics as one matched quantitative conversion rate.');
study('stavreva2009-rat-liver-response', 'stavreva2009-glucocorticoid-pulses', 'Adrenalectomized freely moving Sprague-Dawley rats and liver',
  'Adult 250-300-g rats undergo bilateral adrenalectomy and jugular cannulation, recover 5 days with corticosterone replacement, and have replacement withdrawn 24 hours before testing. Up to three 100-microgram corticosterone-HBC intravenous boluses are given at 0, 60 and 120 minutes; separate terminal cohorts supply plasma and liver.',
  'Plasma corticosterone, liver nuclear GR DNA-binding assays/ChIP and Per1 nascent RNA.',
  'Hormone replacement elicits transient liver GR engagement and Per1 nascent transcription in the reported temporal pattern.',
  ['Sex is not specified in the inspected live-animal Methods. Terminal cohorts are not repeated liver samples from one animal. Synthetic-oligonucleotide binding, endogenous ChIP and transcription are distinct assays; no stable epigenetic rewrite or complete intact HPA response is measured after adrenalectomy.'], 'primary-experiment', [2]);
observation('rat-hormone-replacement-input', 'Timed systemic corticosterone replacement', 'stavreva2009-rat-liver-response', 'The systemic input is imposed in adrenalectomized animals whose endogenous adrenal corticosterone source has been removed; it is not a direct test of a complete natural stress circuit.');
observation('liver-transcription-response', 'Systemic hormone-to-liver transcription response', 'stavreva2009-rat-liver-response', 'The source measures transient hepatic GR-associated readouts and Per1 nascent RNA after timed circulating corticosterone replacement, supporting a bounded systemic-signal-to-cell-expression case.');
operating('rat-hormone-replacement-input', 'liver-transcription-response', 'stavreva2009-rat-liver-response', 'Timed circulating hormone replacement changes measured liver transcription-related endpoints in the declared rat preparation.', 'Adrenalectomized rat liver with existing GR transcription machinery', 'Specified intravenous corticosterone-HBC boluses', 'Liver nuclear GR binding and Per1 nascent RNA in terminal cohorts', 'No autonomous organ origin, normal intact stress response, universal gene effect or inherited chromatin-state mechanism is asserted.');

// Extracellular supply alters metabolism without implying purposive organismal optimization.
study('muir2017-culture-nutrient-context', 'muir2017-environmental-cystine', 'Cultured A549 human lung-cancer cells',
  'A549 cells are compared in standard RPMI-based medium versus adult bovine serum; reciprocal 3.5-kDa dialysis and cystine addition constrain the nutrient contribution. Eight-hour [U-13C5]glutamine tracing and separate four-day proliferation/CB-839 comparisons have distinct endpoints.',
  'Normalized glutamine-derived carbon labeling and cell-count-derived proliferation under glutaminase inhibition.',
  'Small-molecule environment and extracellular cystine change glutamine contribution to TCA metabolites and CB-839 sensitivity; serum itself is not equivalent to all native human interstitial fluid.',
  ['Whole-medium comparisons change many components. Isotope fractional contribution is not identical to absolute pathway flux or a unique causal requirement for all proliferation.'], 'primary-experiment', [0,2]);
observation('cystine-environment-input', 'Extracellular cystine context', 'muir2017-culture-nutrient-context', 'Specified media exchange/dialysis and cystine addition alter an existing cell environment; this is a physical supply constraint rather than a demonstrated goal-directed top-down instruction.');
observation('nutrient-dependent-metabolic-use', 'Environment-dependent glutamine use', 'muir2017-culture-nutrient-context', 'Changing small-molecule conditions changes measured glutamine labeling and inhibitor-sensitive proliferation in A549 cultures, with labeling and growth retained as separate acquisition endpoints.');
operating('cystine-environment-input', 'nutrient-dependent-metabolic-use', 'muir2017-culture-nutrient-context', 'The specified extracellular nutrient manipulations alter source-bound metabolic and growth readouts.', 'A549 cells with their existing transport/metabolic machinery', 'Defined media, reciprocal dialysis and cystine conditions', 'Eight-hour isotope labeling and separate four-day proliferation comparison', 'No universal absolute-flux law, organ-demand optimum or transfer of drug dependence to every in-vivo tumor is admitted.');
study('muir2017-xct-transport-contrast', 'muir2017-environmental-cystine', 'A549 xCT/SLC7A11 knockdown and resistant-cDNA rescue',
  'Control or two SLC7A11-targeting shRNAs are compared with an shRNA-resistant cDNA rescue. Adult bovine serum with or without 208-micromolar cystine is traced for 8 hours with approximately 33% enriched [U-13C5]glutamine; normalized alpha-ketoglutarate labeling quantifies the source contrast.',
  'Cystine-associated increase in normalized glutamine contribution to alpha-ketoglutarate.',
  'SLC7A11 knockdown reduces the cystine-dependent labeling increment, and resistant-cDNA expression attenuates this knockdown effect.',
  ['Rescue supports a transporter-related contribution within these cells, not exact wild-type equivalence, a universal transporter minimum or a unique unmeasured intracellular glutamate pool mechanism. Separate breast-cell overexpression is not pooled as one matched A549 assay.'], 'primary-experiment', [1,2]);
observation('xct-genetic-input', 'Matched xCT knockdown/rescue input', 'muir2017-xct-transport-contrast', 'The genetic intervention and resistant-cDNA rescue are examined in the same declared A549 environmental contrast, avoiding inference of causality solely from cross-cell-line expression correlations.');
observation('xct-conditioned-labeling-output', 'Transporter-conditioned nutrient response', 'muir2017-xct-transport-contrast', 'Knockdown and resistant rescue change the cystine-dependent labeling increment, supporting conditional environmental sensitivity rather than extracellular cystine acting independently of cell state.');
operating('xct-genetic-input', 'xct-conditioned-labeling-output', 'muir2017-xct-transport-contrast', 'Matched knockdown/rescue changes the measured cystine-dependent glutamine-labeling response.', 'Genetically modified A549 cells under the paired serum/cystine conditions', 'SLC7A11 shRNA and resistant cDNA', 'Eight-hour normalized alpha-ketoglutarate fractional labeling', 'This bounded genetic result is not an isolated whole-organ allocation mechanism or exact rescue equivalence.');
study('muir2017-plasma-cystine', 'muir2017-environmental-cystine', 'Nu/nu mice in the Figure 5A plasma-availability experiment',
  'Administer 2.4 g/kg cystine by oral gavage and measure plasma cystine by GC-MS at the indicated subsequent times. Figure 5A supplies the plasma time course; a complete group-specific animal manifest and relation to the separately sampled tumor cohort are not reconstructed.',
  'Plasma cystine concentration over the source sampling times.',
  'Oral cystine raises the measured plasma concentration for several hours. This availability time course is separate from the single terminal tumor-labeling comparison in Figure 5C.',
  ['The plasma time course is not a time series of tumor metabolism. Age, sex, tumor status and repeated-versus-distinct animal sampling units are not fully resolved for this panel.'], 'primary-experiment', [2]);
observation('plasma-cystine-availability', 'Measured circulating cystine availability', 'muir2017-plasma-cystine', 'The Figure 5A plasma concentration measurements establish a finite availability response after oral dosing; they do not measure the kinetics of label incorporation within tumors.');
study('muir2017-xenograft-cystine', 'muir2017-environmental-cystine', 'A549 tumor-bearing nude mice',
  'Tumor-bearing nu/nu mice receive 2.4 g/kg oral cystine or the comparison condition. Methods specify three 200-microlitre intravenous [U-13C5]glutamine boluses (36.2 mg/mL) at 15-minute intervals, starting 45 minutes after gavage, followed by one terminal plasma/tumor harvest 15 minutes after the last bolus. Results instead state 30 minutes from cystine dosing to tracer administration; this timing conflict remains unresolved. A separate constant-infusion comparison is described as six hours in Results/Figure 1B but 300 minutes in Methods.',
  'Terminal plasma cystine/tracer enrichment and tumor metabolite fractional labeling after the bolus sequence.',
  'Cystine-treated animals have higher terminal tumor labeling of glutamine, glutamate and alpha-ketoglutarate in the reported pre-steady-state endpoint comparison. Figure 5B displays hypothetical labeling trajectories, not an acquired tumor time series.',
  ['A single pre-steady-state harvest cannot distinguish faster labeling kinetics from increased eventual steady-state label contribution, and does not determine absolute flux. Results/Methods timing and continuous-infusion duration discrepancies remain explicit. Tumor growth, therapeutic response, full nutrient delivery and a normal organ homeostat are not measured by this intervention.'], 'primary-experiment', [2]);
